  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_section_1",
      "section_title": "Β-hCG interpretation",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "What the OSCE station may say",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A 27-year-old woman presents with 6 weeks of amenorrhoea and mild lower abdominal pain. Her urine pregnancy test is positive. Transvaginal ultrasound shows neither a definite intrauterine pregnancy nor an ectopic pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Her quantitative serum β-hCG is 1,200 IU/L. Forty-eight hours later, it is 1,500 IU/L."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Your tasks:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpret the β-hCG trend."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the differential diagnoses."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Outline further investigations and follow-up."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain how β-hCG is used in ectopic pregnancy and early pregnancy loss."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE principle: β-hCG is useful for monitoring early pregnancy, but a single value or a particular rise or fall cannot independently determine pregnancy location or guarantee viability."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Because the patient has pain and a pregnancy of unknown location, ectopic pregnancy must remain a consideration until the location and outcome are established."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "What is β-hCG?",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is a hormone produced by trophoblastic tissue after implantation. The beta subunit is measured in serum and urine pregnancy tests."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Quantitative serum β-hCG measures the concentration of the hormone in the blood."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is used to help assess:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Early pregnancy progression."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pregnancy of unknown location."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspected ectopic pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspected miscarriage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Resolution after treatment of ectopic pregnancy or molar pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: β-hCG reflects trophoblastic activity. It does not directly prove that an embryo is viable or that a pregnancy is located inside the uterus."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Understanding the units",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Serum β-hCG is commonly reported in:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "IU/L (international units per litre)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "mIU/mL (milli-international units per millilitre)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These numerical values are equivalent: 1 IU/L = 1 mIU/mL."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For example, a β-hCG result of 1,200 IU/L is numerically equivalent to 1,200 mIU/mL."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reference ranges vary between laboratories, and results should be interpreted alongside gestational age, symptoms and ultrasound findings."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "β-hCG in a normal early pregnancy",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In an early pregnancy that is developing normally, β-hCG generally rises, but the rate of rise slows as the pregnancy progresses."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The traditional statement that β-hCG must double every 48 hours is an oversimplification. A viable pregnancy does not always double its β-hCG in that interval."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Approximate minimum expected rises sometimes used in clinical assessment over 48 hours vary with the starting concentration:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Initial β-hCG",
                    "Approximate minimum rise over 48 hours sometimes used in assessment"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Below 1,500 IU/L",
                      "About 49%"
                    ],
                    [
                      "1,500–3,000 IU/L",
                      "About 40%"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Above 3,000 IU/L",
                      "About 33%"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are clinical reference estimates, not absolute diagnostic cut-offs. Some protocols use a more conservative threshold, such as a 35% rise over 48 hours, to reduce the risk of misclassifying a potentially viable pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A rise below an expected threshold raises concern for an abnormal pregnancy, but it does not distinguish ectopic pregnancy from a failing intrauterine pregnancy."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "How to calculate the percentage change",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the following formula:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "\\[ \\text{Percentage change}= \\frac{\\text{Second value}-\\text{First value}} {\\text{First value}}\\times100 \\]"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_6",
              "group_title": "Example 1: Rising β-hCG",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Initial result = 1,200 IU/L."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Repeat result after 48 hours = 1,500 IU/L."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "\\[ \\frac{1500-1200}{1200}\\times100=25\\% \\]"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The β-hCG has increased by 25% over 48 hours."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: This is a slower rise than expected in many normally developing early pregnancies and raises concern for an abnormal pregnancy, including ectopic pregnancy or a failing intrauterine pregnancy. It does not establish the diagnosis. Clinical review and repeat imaging are required."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_7",
              "group_title": "Example 2: Falling β-hCG",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Initial result = 4,000 IU/L."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Repeat result after 48 hours = 2,400 IU/L."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "\\[ \\frac{2400-4000}{4000}\\times100=-40\\% \\]"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The β-hCG has fallen by 40%."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: This suggests a pregnancy that may be resolving, but it does not by itself prove a completed miscarriage or exclude ectopic pregnancy. Follow-up must continue according to the clinical findings and local protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "The three main β-hCG patterns",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Rising β-hCG"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A rise compatible with early pregnancy development is reassuring but does not prove a viable intrauterine pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy remains possible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Falling β-hCG"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suggests a failing or resolving pregnancy. Continue follow-up until the pregnancy outcome is established or β-hCG reaches the endpoint specified by the clinical pathway."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Plateauing or slowly changing β-hCG"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Raises concern for ectopic pregnancy or persistent trophoblastic tissue. It requires prompt clinical reassessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Pregnancy of unknown location (PUL)",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A pregnancy of unknown location is diagnosed when a pregnancy test is positive but transvaginal ultrasound does not identify a definite intrauterine or extrauterine pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "PUL is a temporary classification, not a final diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Possible outcomes include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A very early intrauterine pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A failing intrauterine pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An ectopic pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A pregnancy that resolves without the location ever being established."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_10",
              "group_title": "Management of a stable patient",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess symptoms and haemodynamic stability."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtain quantitative serum β-hCG."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Repeat β-hCG, commonly after approximately 48 hours."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpret the change alongside the initial ultrasound and clinical history."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Arrange repeat transvaginal ultrasound at an appropriate interval."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provide explicit safety-net instructions and ensure reliable follow-up."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not use a β-hCG trend alone to diagnose ectopic pregnancy or to administer methotrexate without an appropriate diagnostic assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "The β-hCG discriminatory zone",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The discriminatory zone refers to a β-hCG concentration above which an intrauterine gestational sac is expected to be visible on ultrasound in many pregnancies."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "However, it is not a universal threshold. Visibility depends on:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ultrasound equipment and operator experience."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Whether transvaginal or transabdominal imaging is used."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Multiple pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gestational age and individual biological variation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The accuracy of pregnancy dating."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some services use a conservative transvaginal ultrasound threshold as high as approximately 3,500 IU/L when trying to avoid mistakenly classifying a potentially viable intrauterine pregnancy as abnormal."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_12",
              "group_title": "Common OSCE trap",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If β-hCG is above the local discriminatory level and no intrauterine pregnancy is seen, ectopic pregnancy becomes more concerning. However, an empty uterus at that concentration is not, by itself, proof of ectopic pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A stable patient requires careful specialist assessment, repeat imaging and appropriate follow-up. A patient with haemodynamic instability or signs of internal bleeding requires emergency management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "β-hCG in miscarriage",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_13",
              "group_title": "A. Threatened miscarriage",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "β-hCG may rise appropriately if the pregnancy remains viable. Vaginal bleeding alone does not prove pregnancy loss."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_14",
              "group_title": "B. Missed or incomplete miscarriage",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "β-hCG may decline, plateau or occasionally show a less typical pattern. Ultrasound findings and clinical assessment determine whether miscarriage is established."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_15",
              "group_title": "C. Complete miscarriage",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "β-hCG usually declines following the loss, but it may take time to become undetectable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If the location of the original pregnancy was never confirmed, do not assume that an empty uterus and falling β-hCG establish complete miscarriage; ectopic pregnancy must still be excluded through appropriate follow-up."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "β-hCG after ectopic pregnancy treatment",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_16",
              "group_title": "After methotrexate",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In a commonly used single-dose protocol:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measure β-hCG on day 4."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Repeat it on day 7."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A decline of at least 15% between days 4 and 7 is generally expected."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If the decline is inadequate, reassess for additional treatment or surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Continue serial monitoring until resolution according to the local protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "β-hCG can rise between days 1 and 4 after methotrexate, so an early rise alone does not establish treatment failure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe or worsening pain, fainting or collapse requires urgent assessment, even if β-hCG is falling."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_17",
              "group_title": "After surgery",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Follow-up depends on the procedure and clinical findings. After salpingotomy, serial β-hCG is particularly important because persistent trophoblastic tissue may remain. Follow the treating team's plan after salpingectomy as well."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "β-hCG in molar pregnancy",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "β-hCG may be markedly elevated in a molar pregnancy, but there is no single level that confirms the diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "After evacuation:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measure serial β-hCG."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ensure appropriate specialist follow-up."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Investigate a plateauing or rising level for persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advise contraception during surveillance so that a new pregnancy does not confuse interpretation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The required monitoring schedule depends on the type of mole and the applicable specialist protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "When β-hCG results require urgent escalation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not wait for routine repeat testing if the patient has:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe or worsening abdominal pain."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Shoulder-tip pain with suspected internal bleeding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Syncope or collapse."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hypotension, marked tachycardia or poor peripheral perfusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Significant vaginal bleeding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Signs of peritonism."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A patient with suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy requires urgent resuscitation and definitive management, regardless of whether β-hCG is low, falling or unavailable."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "How to report β-hCG results to the examiner",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_20",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A useful structure is:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "State the initial β-hCG concentration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "State the interval and repeat concentration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Calculate the percentage rise or fall."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain what the pattern suggests."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "State what it cannot establish."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Outline the next clinical step."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_21",
              "group_title": "Example",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“The initial β-hCG is 1,200 IU/L and rises to 1,500 IU/L over 48 hours, representing a 25% increase. This is a slower rise than expected in many viable early pregnancies and raises concern for an abnormal pregnancy, including ectopic pregnancy or a failing intrauterine pregnancy. The trend does not establish pregnancy location or viability. I would review the symptoms, arrange appropriate repeat transvaginal ultrasound and serial β-hCG follow-up, and provide clear safety-net advice.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Common examiner questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_22",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Must β-hCG double every 48 hours?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. The expected rise varies with the initial concentration and gestational stage. Doubling is not an absolute requirement for viability."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Can ectopic pregnancy occur with a low β-hCG?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Yes. A low concentration does not exclude ectopic pregnancy or rupture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Does a falling β-hCG exclude ectopic pregnancy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. An ectopic pregnancy may have falling β-hCG and can still rupture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What does a plateauing β-hCG suggest?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It raises concern for ectopic pregnancy or persistent trophoblastic tissue and requires clinical reassessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the discriminatory zone?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is a concentration above which an intrauterine gestational sac is expected to be visible in many pregnancies on a particular ultrasound technique. It is not an absolute diagnostic cut-off."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Can a single β-hCG result establish pregnancy viability?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It must be interpreted alongside ultrasound, symptoms, pregnancy dates and serial results when indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE mistakes",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_23",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Saying β-hCG must double every 48 hours."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diagnosing ectopic pregnancy from one β-hCG result."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assuming falling β-hCG excludes rupture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Treating the discriminatory zone as an absolute threshold."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Forgetting the patient's symptoms and haemodynamic status."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Failing to arrange follow-up for pregnancy of unknown location."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Giving methotrexate without adequate diagnostic assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Failing to explain emergency warning signs."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Memory aid: TREND",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_24",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "T — Take the initial quantitative value."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "R — Repeat at the appropriate interval."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "E — Evaluate the percentage change."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "N — Never interpret the result without symptoms and ultrasound."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D — Determine the follow-up plan and give safety-net advice."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_subsection_17",
          "subsection_title": "Quick self-test",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "beta_hcg_interpretation_group_25",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. A patient's β-hCG rises by 25% over 48 hours. What is the most appropriate interpretation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Ectopic pregnancy is confirmed",
                    "Pregnancy loss is confirmed"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The rise is concerning but does not establish location or viability"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "The pregnancy is definitely viable"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Does falling β-hCG exclude a ruptured ectopic pregnancy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Yes",
                    "No",
                    "Only if the initial value was below 1,000 IU/L",
                    "Only if there is no vaginal bleeding"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. A patient has a positive pregnancy test and an empty uterus on ultrasound. What does this establish?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A confirmed ectopic pregnancy",
                    "A confirmed complete miscarriage",
                    "Pregnancy of unknown location, if neither location is identified",
                    "A confirmed molar pregnancy"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. After single-dose methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy, what decline is generally expected between days 4 and 7?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "At least 5%",
                    "At least 10%",
                    "At least 15%",
                    "Exactly 50%"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}